::ENG3_U01_L05_GP_Q01::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision correctly fixes the capitalization in this analytical sentence? Draft: the phrase "unfinished promise" suggests responsibility. {
=The phrase "unfinished promise" suggests responsibility.#Correct. The first word of the sentence is capitalized, and the quoted phrase remains accurate.
~the phrase "unfinished promise" suggests responsibility.#This keeps the capitalization error. A complete analytical sentence should begin with a capital letter.
~The phrase unfinished promise suggests responsibility.#This fixes capitalization but removes the quotation marks around the exact phrase.
~The phrase "unfinished promise" Suggests responsibility.#This creates a new capitalization error by capitalizing a word in the middle of the sentence without a reason.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_GP_Q02::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision best fixes the sentence boundary? Draft: The narrator describes the room as "bright but airless" it feels uncomfortable. {
~The narrator describes the room as "bright but airless" it feels uncomfortable.#This is still a run-on because two complete ideas are joined without punctuation or a connector.
=The narrator describes the room as "bright but airless," so it feels uncomfortable.#Correct. The comma and connector show how the description leads to the explanation.
~The narrator describes the room as "bright but airless" and uncomfortable it feels.#This changes the meaning and creates an awkward sentence.
~The narrator describes the room as "bright but airless" it, feels uncomfortable.#This places punctuation in a confusing location and does not fix the sentence boundary.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_GP_Q03::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which sentence uses punctuation around quoted evidence most clearly? {
~The phrase waiting faces suggests pressure because the narrator feels watched.#The idea is understandable, but the exact phrase should be placed in quotation marks.
~The phrase "waiting faces suggests pressure because the narrator feels watched.#This has an opening quotation mark but no closing quotation mark.
=The phrase "waiting faces" suggests pressure because the narrator feels watched.#Correct. The exact phrase is quoted, and the explanation is connected clearly.
~The phrase "waiting faces", suggests pressure because the narrator feels watched.#The comma after the quoted phrase interrupts the subject and verb.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_GP_Q04::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision turns the fragment into a complete analytical sentence? Fragment: Because the image suggests hidden change. {
~Because the image suggests hidden change.#This is still a fragment because it begins with because but does not complete the thought.
~The image because suggests hidden change.#This is not grammatically clear and does not create a correct sentence.
~Because hidden change in the image.#This is also a fragment because it does not contain a complete idea.
=The image suggests hidden change because the river keeps moving under the ice.#Correct. The revision includes a complete idea and explains the reason.
}

::ENG3_U01_L05_GP_Q05::[MLA.ENG3.L.01] Which revision best fixes the run-on sentence? Draft: The writer uses precise diction it creates a serious tone. {
~The writer uses precise diction it creates a serious tone.#This is still a run-on because two complete ideas are joined without punctuation or a connector.
=The writer uses precise diction, which creates a serious tone.#Correct. The revision connects the ideas clearly and shows how diction affects tone.
~The writer uses precise diction, it creates a serious tone.#This is a comma splice because a comma alone cannot join two complete sentences.
~The writer uses precise diction creates a serious tone.#This removes a needed subject and makes the sentence unclear.
}
